WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.420
When you think of a sculpture,&nbsp;
you probably imagine one of these.&nbsp;

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.951
Would you ever imagine something that&nbsp;
can fit in the eye of a needle?

00:00:07.295 --> 00:00:08.564
Huh?

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.182
Meet Willard Wigan.

00:00:12.182 --> 00:00:14.880
I'm Dr Willard Wigan MBE.
I make the smallest sculptures in the world.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.133
I've made fourteen camels in the eye of a needle...

00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:23.025
The Queen's carriage

00:00:23.025 --> 00:00:25.654
And I've made one of the Queen herself

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:26.522
Mount Rushmore

00:00:26.622 --> 00:00:28.068
Sunseeker Yacht

00:00:28.068 --> 00:00:29.074
Evolution of man

00:00:29.074 --> 00:00:30.719
Marie-Antoinette

00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:32.362
Little Red Riding Hood

00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:34.108
Einstein, and more.

00:00:34.418 --> 00:00:37.097
I'd be here forever trying to tell you.&nbsp;
I've done that many you know.

00:00:37.186 --> 00:00:39.528
It all began with an ant's nest.

00:00:39.528 --> 00:00:44.280
I thought the ants were homeless,&nbsp;so I started making them tables and chairs.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:52.126
I started picking up little fragments of splinters&nbsp;and slicing them and constructing and building&nbsp;little houses. 

00:00:52.248 --> 00:00:54.449
I'm autistic and in school they&nbsp;
didn't really care.

00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:57.374
I call it a learning difference - I just learn different to anybody else.

00:00:57.374 --> 00:01:00.840
I started&nbsp;experimenting to see how small I could really go.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.962
How do you make something so beautiful?

00:01:03.962 --> 00:01:08.618
When&nbsp;I'm working on a microscopic level, I have to work between my heartbeat

00:01:08.618 --> 00:01:12.000
because if I don't, the pulse&nbsp;in my finger will cause problems.

00:01:12.166 --> 00:01:15.818
But the tools are very important because they all vary. There's all&nbsp;different types of tools.

00:01:15.818 --> 00:01:17.531
The cutting is the most important thing.

00:01:17.531 --> 00:01:20.479
Slicing and twisting and bending&nbsp;and manipulating.

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:28.000
I use a little broken shard of diamond- smashed- and it's like a little splinter. It looks like a stone age splint being microscopic. 

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.500
So it's like my pulse is a little jackhammer&nbsp;chiselling away until I get the shape I want.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.708
I normally pluck an eyelash out from the corner of&nbsp;my eye

00:01:37.708 --> 00:01:40.320
then I attach it to a little cocktail stick

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.408
and then I paint with it.

00:01:42.408 --> 00:01:44.320
I work on five at&nbsp;a time.

00:01:44.465 --> 00:01:46.480
If I work on one it'll drive me mad.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:47.080
[Music]

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.869
I have two world records.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.551
The smallest sculptures&nbsp;ever made by human hands

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:56.095
not no machine no nanotechnology.

00:01:56.295 --> 00:01:58.954
But these micro sculptures don't&nbsp;
always go to plan.

00:01:59.234 --> 00:02:02.000
I remember once I was making a sculpture of Alice in Wonderland.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.874
After I'd&nbsp;made the Mad Hatter and I made the teapot, I made Alice separately.

00:02:05.874 --> 00:02:10.365
And I was lifting her, put&nbsp;
her into the needle, and my mobile phone went off.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:11.658
I went like this.

00:02:11.658 --> 00:02:13.579
Who's that?

00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:18.105
And I inhaled Alice. It just went. She's in my cavity somewhere.

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:20.306
Gone.

00:02:21.572 --> 00:02:24.448
The work that I do, it doesn't belong to&nbsp;
me.

00:02:24.448 --> 00:02:27.718
I like other people seeing it. I like to sit&nbsp;there and watch them.

00:02:27.718 --> 00:02:33.036
Some people burst into&nbsp;tears when they see my work, you know, and that brings it home to me what I've done.

00:02:33.147 --> 00:02:35.716
The time that&nbsp;I've put in has been worth it.

00:02:35.716 --> 00:02:37.273
So what next for Willard?

00:02:37.362 --> 00:02:40.635
I know he's stuck in the needle, yeah,&nbsp;
that's a good one.

00:02:40.635 --> 00:02:42.155
I'm gonna do that you know.

